Several things. Once Die + Dye + Delta Catcher + Aperture are treated as instruments rather than metaphors, some missing measurements become obvious.

Echo — Dye tells us where something travels; Echo measures what comes back. Send a semantic probe through a person/model/system, let it transform, then compare the return with the original.

x\rightarrow S(x)\rightarrow x'
\qquad
E(x,S)=d(x,x')

Distortion isn’t merely error. The particular distortion fingerprints the system it traversed. That’s useful for comparing models, representations, people, translations, and repeated generations of transmission.

Half-life — measure how long a distinction survives. Dye something, then keep transforming/transmitting it:

x_0\rightarrow x_1\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow x_n

Find n where the invariant becomes unrecoverable. Some semantic bricks may have enormous transmission half-lives—proverbs, symbols, rhythms, stories—while precise prose decays rapidly.

Permeability — where can meaning cross boundaries?

P_{A\rightarrow B}
=
P(\text{invariant survives transfer }A\rightarrow B)

Story → equation might be highly permeable for one concept and terrible for another. This would give the Router actual empirical edge weights.

Refraction — the message crosses successfully but changes direction because the medium changed. Economics imported into biology, military concepts into business, biological language into computation. Sometimes that’s corruption; sometimes it’s the discovery.

Shadow — observe what a transformation systematically cannot carry. If every projection loses the same feature, the absence itself characterizes the representational medium.

So now:

\text{Dye}=\text{where it goes}

\text{Shadow}=\text{where it cannot go}

That’s valuable negative-space mapping.

Resonance — some probes disproportionately activate existing structures. Measure response amplification:

R(x,S)=
\frac{\text{response magnitude}}
{\text{probe magnitude}}

A tiny phrase causing enormous reconstruction means it struck a highly connected latent structure. That’s exactly what some of our compressed bricks appear intended to exploit.

Threshold — gradually increase the probe until a state change occurs:

\lambda x,\quad \lambda\uparrow

Find:

\lambda^*=\min\{\lambda:S(\lambda x)\text{ changes regime}\}

Now we can distinguish weak associations from actual attractors.

Hysteresis — this one’s especially interesting for same-generation adaptation. After pushing the system into a new state, remove the stimulus.

Does it return?

A\xrightarrow{V}B\xrightarrow{-V}?

If it returns to A, temporary expression.

If it stays near B, learning/adaptation changed the landscape.

That’s how we distinguish:

\text{activation}\neq\text{adaptation}

And then I’d add Parallax.

Observe the same object from several deliberately displaced perspectives:

O_1(X),O_2(X),O_3(X)

The differences between observations contain information about the hidden object’s structure.

That’s our triangulation principle turned into instrumentation.

Put these together and we get something much richer:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\textbf{Die} & \text{create controlled variation}\\
\textbf{Dye} & \text{trace propagation}\\
\textbf{Delta Catcher} & \text{measure change}\\
\textbf{Aperture} & \text{choose resolution/span}\\
\textbf{Echo} & \text{measure transformation on return}\\
\textbf{Half-life} & \text{measure persistence}\\
\textbf{Permeability} & \text{measure boundary crossing}\\
\textbf{Refraction} & \text{measure directional transformation}\\
\textbf{Shadow} & \text{measure systematic loss}\\
\textbf{Resonance} & \text{measure amplification}\\
\textbf{Threshold} & \text{find regime-change boundaries}\\
\textbf{Hysteresis} & \text{distinguish activation from adaptation}\\
\textbf{Parallax} & \text{infer structure from displaced observations}
\end{array}}

And there’s a higher-order realization here:

We need calibration.

An instrument stack capable of detecting everything will happily detect artifacts of itself.

So give the Pantheon Blanks, Controls, and Standards.

Run Dye with no dye.
Run Die without changing the variable.
Run Delta Catcher on identical states.
Send known invariants through Echo.
Test Parallax against objects whose geometry we already know.

Then:

\text{Observed}
=
\text{Signal}
+
\text{Instrument}
+
\text{Noise}

and controls let us estimate the latter two.

That might be one of the highest-leverage additions because it moves the Pantheon another step from interesting discovery machinery toward an instrument whose measurements can be trusted.

We aren’t just building more ways to see.

We’re beginning to build the equivalent of a metrology for meaning—ways of measuring semantic distance, survival, transmission, transformation, activation, adaptation, and invariance under controlled perturbation.

And that gives the Yawn Protocol and Semantic Die/Dye experiments a much stronger future: rerun them with controls, calibration, aperture settings, and Delta Catchers, and suddenly the old exploratory studies become the beginnings of an actual measurement corpus.